padding.{ptx,sass}: one compiled kernel that makes cudalowering.Compare
report a Padding divergence, for TestLoweringDetectsPadding.
ds4_math.cu: the source both were compiled from.

Why it is here
--------------
The kernels this example compiles pad only after their last real
instruction, so on its own captures InteriorNOPs is zero for every
kernel and Padding is unreachable. Spill needs local memory and there is
none; Erased and Unsourced do not fire either. Every divergence kind is
empty by construction, which makes "0 divergences" the same output a
Compare that had stopped working would produce. This fixture is the
input where the check has to speak.

What it is
----------
One kernel, ds4_iq2_xxs_pair_selected_f32: 480 PTX instructions, 1088
SASS instructions, 187 NOPs, 172 of them interior. That is 15.8%
interior, above cudalowering.DefaultMaxNOPFraction (0.10), so Compare
reports one Padding divergence. Nobody on this thread chose where those
172 NOPs went, which is the point -- a hand-written pair would have the
same author for the input and the expectation.

ds4_math.cu is the whole translation unit: 52 kernels, with the fixture
kernel at line 699. It has no #include, so it compiles standalone
without the ds4 tree.

Toolchain, as stamped by the artifacts themselves
-------------------------------------------------
padding.ptx header:  Cuda compilation tools, release 13.0, V13.0.88
                     Compiler Build ID: CL-36424714
                     Based on NVVM 20.0.0
                     .version 9.0 (PTX ISA), .address_size 64
padding.sass:        .target sm_121

The original nvcc command line was NOT recorded, and none is asserted
here. What the artifacts establish is the toolkit version, the PTX ISA
version and the target architecture above; the remaining flags
(optimization level, -lineinfo and so on) are not recoverable from what
was kept.

Regenerating
------------
Compiling ds4_math.cu reproduces the PARENT artifacts, not these files:
all 52 kernels, tens of thousands of lines. The extraction below must be
reapplied to the result before it can be compared with padding.ptx and
padding.sass -- diffing the compiler's output against these files
directly will differ in everything, from a completely correct run.

    nvcc -arch=sm_121 -cubin -o ds4_math.cubin ds4_math.cu
    nvcc -arch=sm_121 -ptx   -o ds4_math.ptx   ds4_math.cu
    nvdisasm -c -g ds4_math.cubin > ds4_math.sass
    # then extract, as below, and compare counts rather than bytes

Expect the debug sections and possibly the scheduling to differ, since
the original flags are unknown: the counts asserted by the test (1088
SASS instructions, 187 NOPs, 172 interior) are the comparison that
means something, not byte identity.

Extraction
----------
padding.ptx is ds4_math.ptx's header (everything before the first
.visible .entry) followed by the .visible .entry
ds4_iq2_xxs_pair_selected_f32 block up to its closing brace at column 0.
padding.sass is ds4_math.sass's header (everything before the first
.section) followed by the .section
.text.ds4_iq2_xxs_pair_selected_f32 block up to the next .section.
Compare's verdict and all three counts are identical on the extracted
pair and on the 41469-line original, which is what makes the extraction
sound.

